WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.639 --> 00:00:18.650
When the exhausted straw hats are at the verge
of death, cornered at the port of Ennis Lobby

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:23.570
by the countless enormous warships of the
arriving buster call, threatening to obliterate

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:31.130
the entire island, at the last moment, they
are saved by none other than their ship. Seeing

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:36.040
the pirate boat arrive is a heartwarming and
tearjerking scene that brings the adventure

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:42.230
of Ennis Lobby to a worthy end. It just feels
so cathartic that the ship has come to protect

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:50.280
her crew and sail with them all one last time.
But… why? How is it that we have build a

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:57.469
relationship with not a character we love
but…a ship? It’s an easy enough question,

00:00:57.469 --> 00:01:01.879
but after sitting down to make this video,
I had to find out the hard way that it actually

00:01:01.879 --> 00:01:08.660
has a really difficult answer. And that answer
is Anthropomorphism. So, once you will have

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:12.550
watched this video and figured out how to
pronounce this word, you will not only be

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:17.070
able to use this technique to improve your
own stories, but also have learned a great

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:22.110
new word you can annoy all your friends and
family with. So let’s explore how the going

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:27.350
merry seamlessly unites a number of story
telling devices to create the perfect adventure

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:32.750
story. To understand the entirety of how Oda
turned a mere boat into an ingenious story

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:39.299
element, we have to look at it from two perspectives:
The Going Merry as a ship and the Going Merry

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:45.810
as character. So what makes the Going merry
special? And what are these devices and techniques

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:50.990
YOU can use to take an everyday object and
enhance your entire story with it, to get

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:54.659
that emotional reaction from your audience?

00:01:54.659 --> 00:02:02.700
I think, before we look at how to turn an
object into a character, a great place to

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:08.229
start is to understand the true potential
objects in themselves hold. You see, no matter

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:14.140
what type of story you write, chances are
that they will not only consist of people.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:19.630
Characters wear cloths, swing swords, drive
cars and eat food. So while all these things

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:24.519
can simply be part of your story for the sake
of illustration and realism, instead of seeing

00:02:24.519 --> 00:02:29.920
them as necessary accessories to your writing,
why not make use of these items and make them

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.190
part of the experience. To illustrate this
point, let’s return to One Piece. Because

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:40.540
the Merry as a ship actually carries a lot
of meaning and symbolism that functions as

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:46.130
a framework for the pirate story Oda is writing.
First of all , the Going Merry is portraed

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:52.420
not as a mere vessel for transportation, but
as a home for the entire straw hat crew. The

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:57.180
ship is drawn comfortable and inviting and
thus creates a certain atmosphere in which

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:04.220
the crew can relax, socialise and live together.
It is a space associated with positive experiences

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:10.040
and bonding moments for the main characters.
Secondly , the design of the ship gives additional

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:15.819
characterisation to the story. Despite being
a pirate ship, the bright and friendly appearance

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:21.989
of the Merry and most of its rooms immediately
tells us what sort of people live here. This

00:03:21.989 --> 00:03:27.549
is further enhanced by details like the smiling
lamb figurehead or Nami´s Tangerine trees

00:03:27.549 --> 00:03:33.470
that have been planted on deck. Even without
knowing anything about the straw hats, simply

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:39.310
looking at their ship alone would already
tell us that these are good and friendly people.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:44.760
And of course having a Jolly Roger on its
sail, we also keep being reminded that this

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:51.569
is a pirate crew traveling the seas. As a
result, the ship and the crew are SO closely

00:03:51.569 --> 00:03:56.900
connected, that when you see the Merry, you
can’t help but think about the straw hats.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:03.220
Thirdly , the ship determines the space of
our story by carrying Luffy and co. from adventure

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:10.090
to adventure and closer to their overall goal.
Spending time on the ship almost always tells

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:15.239
us that we are about to change location and
start a new plot threat. In a way, it also

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:20.579
functions as a sort of intermezzo in between
arcs, where the crew and also the audience

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:26.330
can relax and recover from the climax of the
previous arc. And finally , the Merry as a

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:32.580
ship also carries a lot of symbolism in the
story. Or maybe I should say, it is filled

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:39.700
to the brim with symbolism for both crew and
reader alike. The pirate flag is a symbol

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:45.350
for the pirate story we are reading, the tangerine
trees are a reminder of Nami´s past and home,

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:50.880
same as the Merry itself is a reminder for
Usopp, just to name a few. On a larger scale

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.830
however, the ship symbolises the spirit of
freedom and adventure that defines One Piece

00:04:55.830 --> 00:05:01.710
as a story, and the increasing damage it takes
serves as a reminder for all the adventures

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:07.820
the crew has been on and all the places they
traveled to. So, as you see, even when looking

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:13.440
at it as nothing more than a ship, the Merry
already adds quite a lot to the story. And

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.270
you can be sure that Oda has sunken a lot
of time into its design. Using an object to

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:24.630
create atmosphere, characterisation, motion
and symbolism is a very effective way of drawing

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:29.750
your audience deeper into the story and make
them want to see more of it. And this can

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:35.849
be applied to any object you can think of,
like a piece of wood, or a ring or even a

00:05:35.849 --> 00:05:42.000
chair, to create an immense emotional response
in your audience. But there is even more that

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:48.120
you can do with an object. You can actually
make it into a character itself.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:54.770
I’m definitely not the first one that calls
the Going Merry a part of the straw hat crew.

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:59.590
And I strongly believe that the Merry as a
crew member does a wonderful job at enhancing

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:04.620
the characters and the plot of the story.
But how exactly can you give characterisation

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:10.210
to a ship, or any object at that? This is
where the wonderful term from the beginning

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:17.719
of this video comes into play: Anthropomorphism.
So what exactly is that? Anthropomorphism

00:06:17.719 --> 00:06:24.039
is a literary device that assigns human characteristics
to nonhuman entities like animals or inanimate

00:06:24.039 --> 00:06:25.039
objects. So how is this different from personification?
I’m glad you asked! The difference between

00:06:25.039 --> 00:06:26.039
anthropomorphism and personification is that
Personification, is the use of figurative

00:06:26.039 --> 00:06:27.039
language to give inanimate objects or natural
phenomena humanlike characteristics&nbsp;in a

00:06:27.039 --> 00:06:28.039
metaphorical and representative way, for instance
the howling wind or the biting cold. Anthropomorphism,

00:06:28.039 --> 00:06:29.039
on the other hand, involves non-human things
displaying literal human traits and being

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:30.039
capable of human behaviour. So, now you might
think, well great, but why would you want

00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:35.690
to do this? Because, if done right, it introduces
something unusual and thus captures the readers

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:41.970
interest and empathy. Anthropomorphism is
actually quite popular these days and anime

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:47.920
and manga are no exception for this. In the
case of the Going Merry, I’m of course talking

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.170
about the ships spirit that takes on human
appearance in the form of the Klabautermann.

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:57.160
(German approved stamp) As Franky explains
to Usopp, this is a rare manifestation of

00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:03.500
a ship's soul made possible by the love and
care given to a ship by its crew. Thus, the

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:09.740
small figure Usopp witnesses repairing the
broken Ship on Skypea is actually Marry herself.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:16.810
In other words, the Merry being alive is not
a metaphor. She can actually communicate with

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:22.000
people. And this is also not exclusive to
the straw hats. When Merry wants to save her

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:28.280
crew from Ennis Lobby she asks Iceberg to
repair her for one last journey. This, I think,

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:34.310
is important. Because while Merry gains human-like
characteristics, she still shows mostly traits

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:40.370
and interests associated with a ship and thus,
focuses on being a great pirate ship, repairing

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:46.700
itself and carrying its crew through any hardship.
What then, is the consequence of this technique.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:51.860
Well, the first thing you will notice, is
that when humanising an object it can start

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:57.200
forming relationships with your characters,
or heck, it can even become a main character

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:03.700
itself. The Merry has formed a bond with all
of the straw hats, but Luffy and Usopp in

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:10.589
particular actually communicate with her.
Usopp promising to repair her and Luffy apologising

00:08:10.589 --> 00:08:16.810
for not taking better care of her. Secondly,
the Going Merry offers a completely new direction

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:22.200
for the plot line. She is the cause for stopping
at Water 7 in the first place, to look for

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:26.220
a shipwright that might be able to repair
her, and thus triggering the entire Water

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:32.279
7 and Ennis Lobby saga, which I shamelessly
admit is my favourite in the entire story.

00:08:32.279 --> 00:08:38.710
But even more creatively, Oda actually uses
Merry to safe the crew from Ennis Lobby. This,

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:43.820
I believe, is an especially strong moment,
because the straw hats just declared war against

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:49.120
the world government to save Robin. Despite
being fought over and ultimately abandoned,

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:54.770
she had such a strong connection to the drew
that she was compelled to ask iceberg to fix

00:08:54.770 --> 00:09:01.060
her so she could save her crew one last time
and against all odds. Having that love and

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:06.570
loyalty rewarded by their ship, that came
alive through these emotions, is just a very

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:12.860
powerful moment. And so lastly, the merry
functions as a link between all of the straw

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:18.650
hats. She was the place they all forged their
relationships on and got to travel together.

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:23.350
And thus, we really feel it in the end, when
Merry gets her Viking funeral and has to say

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:30.040
goodbye to her crew. It’s not just feeling
sad for a ship. It’s feeling sad for a character.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:34.460
And in the end, we even get some catharsis
with the spirit of the Merry being able to

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:36.740
keep accompanying the crew on the rest of
their journey.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:37.740
Now, if you want to replicate this in your
story, I have three simple steps for you,

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:38.740
that you can follow. Oh and by the way, I’m
curious, is writing a story something you’ve

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:39.740
ever considered? Let me know in the comments.
So, going back, these three steps to use Anthropomorphism

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:40.740
in your story are: One, think about animals
or objects that interest you. Consider whether

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:41.740
there are certain animals or inanimate objects
that seem compelling to you that could be

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:42.740
anthropomorphised for the purpose of your
story. Two, Reflect on visual traits or behaviour.

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:43.740
If you’ve chosen an animal for instance,
what emotional qualities does this animal

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:44.740
seem to possess? If you’ve chosen an object,
does its shape or structure evoke any human

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:45.740
characteristics? And three, Combine both human
and inhuman elements. Some of the most imaginative

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:46.740
and successful examples of anthropomorphism
involve combining real traits of an animal

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:47.740
or object with more human-like characteristics.
Think about ways that you can utilise behaviours

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:48.740
that this new character might have whenever
it is in its natural state.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:49.740
That’s it for today guys. If you enjoy One
Piece and want to learn more about story-telling,

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:50.740
world-building or my thoughts on the latest
One Piece chapter, make sure to like and subscribe

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:54.500
to become part of the channel right now. I
hope to see you around next time. And. Until

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:57.320
then. Stay safe and have a wonderful week.

